Security Audit Report: Reentrancy & Access Control Review: Gemini Target Protocol : Gemini (TVL: $5369.8M) Security Audit Report – Reentrancy & Access‑Control Review Protocol: Gemini (TVL ≈ $5.37 B across Ethereum & L2s) Date: 23 Oct 2026 Auditor: Senior DeFi Security Researcher – OpenAI Security Labs 1. Executive Summary Gemini is a high‑value, cross‑chain liquidity hub that aggregates deposits, executes swaps, and provides lending/borrowing services on Ethereum mainnet and several L2 roll‑ups (Optimism, Arbitrum, zkSync). The platform’s total value locked (TVL) of ~$5.37 B makes it a prime target for sophisticated adversaries. Our focused audit examined reentrancy and access‑control mechanisms across the core contracts: Component Primary Function Criticality (1‑5) GeminiVault Custody of user deposits, ERC‑4626 share logic 5 GeminiRouter Cross‑chain swap routing, fee distribution 4 GeminiLending Collateral management, loan issuance 5 GeminiAdmin Governance & emergency pause 5 GeminiBridge (L2 adapters) Asset lock/unlock across roll‑ups 4 Key Findings Category Findings Severity Reentrancy • Several external‑call patterns (e.g., transfer , call , delegatecall ) are performed before state updates in GeminiVault.withdraw , GeminiLending.repay , and GeminiRouter.swapExactTokensForTokens . • L2 bridge contracts use a pull‑based unlock that can be re‑entered via malicious token callbacks. High (8/10) Access‑Control • onlyOwner is used on many functions, but the owner is a multisig with a single‑signer threshold (1‑of‑3) – insufficient for a $5 B protocol. • GeminiAdmin.pause() can be called by any address flagged as PAUSER_ROLE which is granted to a single external contract that is upgradeable without a timelock. • Missing role revocation checks on grantRole / revokeRole allow an attacker who compromises a role admin to permanently seize admin rights. Critical (9/10) Combined A reentrancy exploit on withdraw combined with a compromised PAUSER_ROLE could freeze the vault, drain funds, and prevent the community from reacting. Critical (9/10) Overall risk score: 8.7 / 10 (rounded to 9 ). The protocol’s size and the presence of high‑severity reentrancy and governance weaknesses warrant immediate remediation. 2. Identified Attack Vectors 2.1 Reentrancy Vulnerabilities # Contract / Function Vulnerability Description Exploit Scenario 1 GeminiVault.withdraw(uint256 shares) Calls IERC20(token).transfer(msg.sender, amount) before updating totalShares and the user’s shareBalance . An attacker deploys a malicious ERC‑20 token that re‑enters withdraw via the ERC‑777 tokensReceived hook, repeatedly pulling out more shares than owned. 2 GeminiLending.repay(uint256 loanId, uint256 amount) Performs token.transferFrom(msg.sender, address(this), amount) then updates loan.principal . A malicious token’s transferFrom triggers a callback to repay again, allowing the attacker to repay a loan multiple times while only paying once, creating a negative debt that can be withdrawn later. 3 GeminiRouter.swapExactTokensForTokens(uint256 amountIn, …) Executes tokenIn.transferFrom(msg.sender, address(this), amountIn) → router.swap(...) → tokenOut.transfer(msg.sender, amountOut) before updating internal fee accounting. An attacker’s token implements transferFrom that calls back into swapExactTokensForTokens , inflating the amountOut received while the fee state remains unchanged, resulting in fee theft. 4 GeminiBridge.unlock(address token, uint256 amount, address to) (L2 adapters) Uses a pull‑based unlock ( token.transfer(to, amount) ) after emitting a UnlockRequested event but before marking the request as completed . A malicious token’s transfer callback re‑enters unlock , causing the same request to be processed multiple times, effectively minting assets on the L2 side. 5 GeminiAdmin.upgradeTo(address newImplementation) (UUPS proxy) Calls newImplementation.initialize() after setting the implementation pointer. If the new implementation contains a re‑entrancy‑prone initialize , an attacker can re‑enter the upgrade process and replace the implementation with a malicious contract before the upgrade finishes. 2.2 Access‑Control Weaknesses # Contract / Function Weakness Potential Impact A GeminiAdmin.pause() / unpause() PAUSER_ROLE granted to GeminiBridgeAdapter (upgradeable via proxyAdmin without timelock). Compromise of the adapter (e.g., via a malicious upgrade) lets an attacker pause the entire protocol, freezing withdrawals and swaps. B onlyOwner on critical functions ( setFee , setInterestRate , addSupportedToken ) Owner is a 1‑of‑3 multisig; no time‑delay or secondary confirmation. Single key compromise leads to immediate fee manipulation, token whitelist changes, or arbitrary fund transfers. C grantRole / revokeRole in GeminiAccessControl No renounceRole restriction for DEFAULT_ADMIN_ROLE ; any admin can grant itself admin rights. An attacker who gains any role can elevate to admin and seize governance. D GeminiLending.setCollateralFactor(address token, uint256 factor) No on‑chain verification that token is a price‑feed‑supported asset. Malicious token can be set with a 100 % collateral factor, enabling under‑collateralized borrowing. E GeminiRouter.setRouter(address newRouter) No onlyOwner check; uses onlyGovernor which is a role that can be granted by any existing governor. Governance takeover via role escalation leads to routing to a malicious contract that siphons fees. 2.3 Combined Attack Path Compromise a low‑privilege role (e.g., PAUSER_ROLE ) via a malicious upgrade of the bridge adapter. Pause the protocol, preventing users from withdrawing. Exploit reentrancy in GeminiVault.withdraw using a malicious ERC‑777 token to repeatedly call withdraw while the contract is paused (the pause flag is checked after the external call). Drain the vault of all shares belonging to the attacker’s address, leaving the rest of users locked. The synergy of a pause‑bypass and reentrancy makes this a high‑impact, low‑complexity attack vector. 3. Prioritized Technical Recommendations Priority Recommendation Rationale Implementation Sketch P1 Apply the Checks‑Effects‑Interactions (CEI) pattern to every external call in GeminiVault , GeminiLending , GeminiRouter , and bridge contracts. Eliminates classic reentrancy windows. solidity
function withdraw(uint256 shares) external {
uint256 amount = _previewWithdraw(shares);
_updateShares(msg.sender, shares); // effect
token.safeTransfer(msg.sender, amount); // interaction
}
| | P1 | Introduce a reentrancy guard ( nonReentrant modifier from OpenZeppelin) on all state‑changing external functions that perform token transfers or external calls. | Provides a second line of defense if CEI is missed. | Add import "@openzeppelin/contracts/security/ReentrancyGuard.sol"; and inherit. | | P1 | Upgrade the governance multisig to a 2‑of‑3 (or higher) threshold with a timelock (e.g., 48‑hour delay) for any onlyOwner actions. | Reduces single‑key compromise risk. | Deploy a TimelockController and set it as the new owner . | | P2 | Restrict pause/unpause to a dedicated, timelocked role ( PAUSE_ADMIN_ROLE ) that is separate from any upgradeable contract. | Prevents accidental or malicious pausing via compromised adapters. | solidity
bytes32 public constant PAUSE_ADMIN_ROLE = keccak256("PAUSE_ADMIN_ROLE");
function pause() external onlyRole(PAUSE_ADMIN_ROLE) { _pause(); }
| | P2 | Add a “post‑upgrade initialization guard” – call initialize before setting the new implementation pointer, or use a two‑step upgrade ( upgradeToAndCall ) with a reentrancy lock. | Stops re‑entrancy during upgrades. | solidity
function upgradeTo(address newImpl) external onlyRole(UPGRADER_ROLE) {
require(!upgrading, "upgrade in progress");
upgrading = true;
_setImplementation(newImpl);
(bool success,) = newImpl.delegatecall(abi.encodeWithSignature("initialize()"));
require(success, "init failed");
upgrading = false;
}
| | P2 | Validate token whitelist changes – require that any token added to the system has a verified price feed (Chainlink or similar) and a minimum liquidity threshold. | Prevents malicious tokens with 100 % collateral factor. | Add require(isSupportedPriceFeed(token), "No price feed") in addSupportedToken . | | P3 | Implement role‑renunciation and admin‑role separation – DEFAULT_ADMIN_ROLE should be assigned to a timelocked DAO contract, not to any externally owned address. | Stops role‑escalation attacks. | Use OpenZeppelin AccessControl with grantRole restricted to ADMIN_ROLE that is itself timelocked. | | P3 | Add “pause‑aware” checks – ensure that any function that performs external calls first checks the pause flag before the call, and reverts if paused. | Guarantees that pausing truly halts all external interactions. | solidity
modifier whenNotPaused() { require(!paused(), "Paused"); _; }
function withdraw(...) external whenNotPaused nonReentrant { … }
| | P3 | Deploy a “reentrancy test harness” on a forked mainnet environment that simulates ERC‑777, ERC‑1363, and malicious bridge callbacks to verify that all CEI patterns hold. | Provides empirical confidence before mainnet deployment. | Use Hardhat/Foundry scripts with custom malicious token contracts. | | P4 | Formal verification of the bridge’s state machine – model the lock/unlock flow in a tool like Certora or Slither to prove that a request cannot be marked completed twice. | Guarantees bridge safety across L2s. | Write a Certora rule: assert !request.completed || request.amount == 0 . | | P4 | Introduce a “circuit‑breaker” for fee accounting – if an abnormal fee delta (> 5 % of TVL) is detected within a single block, automatically pause fee‑related functions. | Early detection of fee‑drain attacks. | Add a monitoring contract that watches totalFeesCollected . | Implementation Timeline (Suggested) Week Milestones 1‑2 Apply CEI pattern & nonReentrant to all vulnerable functions. Deploy to testnet. 3‑4 Upgrade governance multisig to 2‑of‑3 + timelock; migrate owner role. 5‑6 Refactor pause logic, create dedicated PAUSE_ADMIN_ROLE , and add pre‑call pause checks. 7‑8 Harden upgrade flow with initialization guard and reentrancy lock. 9‑10 Add token whitelist validation, price‑feed checks, and collateral‑factor caps. 11‑12 Deploy formal verification for bridge, run reentrancy test harness, and conduct a full‑suite audit on a forked mainnet. 13 Mainnet upgrade with a staged rollout (first L2, then Ethereum) and community announcement. 4. Risk Score Dimension Score (1‑10) Comments Reentrancy Exposure 9 Multiple high‑value functions expose external calls before state updates; no guard in place. Access‑Control Exposure 9 Owner/pauser roles are overly centralized; upgradeable contracts can grant privileged roles without delay. Economic Impact 9 Successful exploit could drain > $ 💰 Support & On-Demand Security Audits If you found this vulnerability research or security